RAISE(3)                       P32W Unix                        RAISE(3)

NAME
       raise - send a signal to the calling process

SYNOPSIS
       #include <signal.h>

       int raise(int sig);

DESCRIPTION
       raise sends the signal sig to the process itself: it is
       kill(getpid(), sig). What happens then is as signal(2) says:
       the handler runs, or the signal is ignored, or the process
       ends.

RETURN VALUE
       0. A process may always signal itself, and the kernel
       ignores a signal number outside 1 to 15 without reporting
       it.

EXAMPLES
       raise(SIGTERM);

SEE ALSO
       kill(1), kill(2), signal(2), exit(3)

BUGS
       raise(0) and raise(99) do nothing and return 0: there is no
       EINVAL.
